## 15. FINAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

The application is acceptable only when:

- A new user can register, verify their email, and log in.
- A user can create an event and retrieve it after refreshing or logging in again.
- The real backend and MongoDB persist the event and planning data.
- AI generation uses a configured provider and returns validated structured data.
- A failed AI request does not destroy existing event data.
- Users can manage budgets, expenses, timelines, and tasks.
- Calculations are correct and tested.
- One user cannot access another user's private events.
- Administrative access is enforced server-side.
- The UI works on mobile, tablet, and desktop.
- Forms, buttons, and navigation perform their intended actions.
- Automated tests and production build checks have been executed and reported honestly.
- No secrets are exposed in frontend bundles, logs, or source control.
- Deployment and recovery instructions are documented.

Start by presenting the architecture, MVP scope, folder structure, database design, API contracts, and implementation plan. Then build the project incrementally, verifying each stage before proceeding. If working in a coding environment, inspect the existing project before modifying it, preserve valid existing code, and report which files were created or changed.

Do not attempt to implement every advanced feature in one enormous, unverified code dump. Prioritize a secure, working MVP and expand it systematically.